ed. Nabeel had grown up in the late 1950s and early '60s in a large and loving family, amid the domestic drama typical of Iraq's new middle class, with his mother Sabria working as a seamstress to send all of her seven children to college. As his story unfolds, Nabeel meets his future wife and finds his poetic voice while he is a student. But Saddam's rise to power ushers in a new era of repression, imprisonment and betrayal from which few families will escape intact. In this new climate of intimidation and random violence Iraqis live in fear and silence; yet Nabeel's mother tells him \"It is your duty to write.\" His poetry, a blend of myth and history, attacks the regime determined to silence him. As Nabeel's fame and influence as a poet grows, he is forced into hiding when the Party begins to dismantle the city's infrastructure and impose power cuts and food rationing. Two of his brothers are already in prison and a third is used as a human minesweeper on the frontline of the Iran-Iraq war.","primaryTopic":"Climate, Weather & Disaster","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Memoir & Autobiography","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Memoir & Autobiography","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Memoir & Autobiography","mappedSubject":"Memoir & Autobiography","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book is primarily a first-person account of Nabeel Yasin's experiences as a poet fleeing oppression in Iraq, fitting squarely within Memoir & Autobiography, while also encompassing elements of Biography and the political context of his struggles.","id":"subject-evidence-book-nabeel-s-song-a-family-story-of-survival-in-iraq-jo-tatchell-1"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","rawLabel":"Biography & Autobiography","mappedSubject":"Memoir & Autobiography","score":7,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-nabeel-s-song-a-family-story-of-survival-in-iraq-j